 ## Overview
-The Tabulated BSSRDF method approximates the spatially varying reflectance distribution function (BSSRDF) for subsurface scattering by precomputing a lookup table of the single-scattering profile $S_r$ as a function of albedo $\rho$ and optical radius $r_{optical}$ [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of the high dimensionality of the full BSSRDF, which depends on five parameters ($\eta, g, \rho, \sigma_t, r$), by fixing the refractive index $\eta$ and asymmetry coefficient $g$ and reducing the remaining variables to a 2D table [S1, unofficial]. This approach sets it apart from analytical approximations like the Dipole model by trading storage space for potentially higher accuracy across a wider range of material parameters, while avoiding the computational cost of evaluating complex analytical functions per sample [S1, unofficial].
+The Tabulated BSSRDF method approximates the single-scattering profile $S_r$ for subsurface scattering by precomputing a lookup table as a function of albedo $\rho$ and optical radius $r_{optical}$ [S1, unofficial]. It reduces the full five-dimensional BSSRDF $S_r(\eta, g, \rho, \sigma_t, r)$ by fixing the refractive index $\eta$ and asymmetry coefficient $g$, and merging the extinction coefficient $\sigma_t$ and distance $r$ into the dimensionless optical radius $r_{optical} = \sigma_t r$ [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs:**
-*   **Input:** Distance $r = ||p_o - p_i||$, material parameters: albedo $\rho$, reduced scattering coefficient $\sigma_s'$, absorption coefficient $\sigma_a$, and extinction coefficient $\sigma_t = \sigma_a + \sigma_s'$ (per spectrum channel). The table is precomputed for fixed $\eta$ and $g$.
-*   **Output:** Spectrum $S_r(r)$, the single-scattering contribution at distance $r$.
+*   **Input:** Distance $r = ||p_o - p_i||$, albedo $\rho$, and extinction coefficient $\sigma_t$ per spectrum channel [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Output:** Spectrum $S_r(r)$ representing the single-scattering contribution [S1, unofficial].
 
